<main id="main">

<section class="wrap tiles-section" aria-label="Headline results">
  <ul class="tiles">
    <li class="tile">
      <span class="tile-label">PR-AUC on the untouched holdout</span>
      <span class="tile-value">{{ho_pr_auc}}</span>
      <span class="tile-sub">95% CI {{ho_pr_auc_ci}} · random score &asymp; {{ho_noskill}}</span>
    </li>
    <li class="tile">
      <span class="tile-label">ROC-AUC on the holdout</span>
      <span class="tile-value">{{ho_roc_auc}}</span>
      <span class="tile-sub">95% CI {{ho_roc_auc_ci}}</span>
    </li>
    <li class="tile">
      <span class="tile-label">Fraud caught at the chosen threshold (holdout)</span>
      <span class="tile-value">{{ho_recall}}</span>
      <span class="tile-sub">95% CI {{ho_recall_ci}} · {{ho_alert_rate}} of transactions stepped up</span>
    </li>
    <li class="tile">
      <span class="tile-label">Expected cost cut vs stepping up nobody (CV)</span>
      <span class="tile-value">{{cv_saving}}</span>
      <span class="tile-sub">Illustrative costs · holdout {{ho_saving}} {{ho_saving_ci}}, post-hoc</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="problem" class="wrap prose">
  <h2><span class="num">1</span> The problem</h2>
  <p>A card issuer scores every transaction. Scores above a threshold go to <strong>step-up</strong> (manual review or
    extra authentication such as OTP / 3-D Secure); the rest are approved. Two mistakes cost money in different ways:
    a good customer stepped up (friction and review effort) and a missed fraud (the amount is lost).</p>
  <p>So the goal has two parts: a model that <strong>ranks</strong> fraud well, measured by PR-AUC because only
    {{fraud_rate}} of transactions are fraud, and a <strong>threshold</strong> that minimises total expected cost
    rather than one that maximises an accuracy-style metric.</p>
</section>

<section id="data" class="wrap prose">
  <h2><span class="num">2</span> Data and its limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/uditjain13/credit-card-fraud-detection-2026"><em>Credit Card Fraud
      Detection 2026</em></a> by Udit Jain on Kaggle, released under <strong>CC0</strong>. It is a <strong>public
      dataset of unspecified provenance</strong>: the data card does not say whether rows are real or synthetic, so no
      result here is a claim about real-world card fraud.</li>
    <li>{{n_rows}} transactions, {{n_fraud}} of them fraud ({{fraud_rate}}, 95% CI {{fraud_rate_ci}}), {{n_features}}
      features. {{n_checks_failed}} of {{n_checks}} structural data checks failed.</li>
    <li>Hints of synthetic generation: channel and device look statistically independent (p = {{chan_dev_p}}), and
      the smallest fraud is {{fraud_min_usd}}.</li>
    <li><strong>No customer or card ID and no real timestamp</strong>, so no customer-history features, no grouped
      split and no out-of-time test.</li>
    <li>The split was locked before any label-based analysis: a development set of {{dev_n}} rows ({{dev_fraud}} fraud)
      for 5-fold cross-validation repeated 3 times, and a holdout of {{holdout_n}} rows ({{holdout_fraud}} fraud)
      scored <strong>once</strong> at the end.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="results" class="wrap prose">
  <h2><span class="num">3</span> Main results</h2>
  <p>Final model: <strong>{{final_model}}</strong>. A random score has PR-AUC &asymp; {{noskill}} (the fraud rate);
    the model reaches about {{cv_lift}}&times; that on cross-validation.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="results">
      <caption>Cross-validation on the dev split vs the holdout, scored once. Brackets are 95% confidence intervals
        (CV: across 15 folds; holdout: stratified bootstrap).</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Metric</th><th scope="col">CV on dev</th><th scope="col">Holdout ({{ho_fraud}} fraud)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">PR-AUC (primary)</th><td>{{cv_pr_auc}} <span class="ci">{{cv_pr_auc_ci}}</span></td><td>{{ho_pr_auc}} <span class="ci">{{ho_pr_auc_ci}}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">ROC-AUC</th><td>{{cv_roc_auc}} <span class="ci">{{cv_roc_auc_ci}}</span></td><td>{{ho_roc_auc}} <span class="ci">{{ho_roc_auc_ci}}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Recall at threshold {{threshold}}</th><td>{{cv_recall}}</td><td>{{ho_recall}} <span class="ci">{{ho_recall_ci}}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Precision at that threshold</th><td>{{cv_precision}}</td><td>{{ho_precision}} <span class="ci">{{ho_precision_ci}}</span></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Share stepped up</th><td>{{cv_alert_rate}}</td><td>{{ho_alert_rate}} <span class="ci">{{ho_alert_rate_ci}}</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The holdout agrees with CV within its (wide) intervals. <strong>Logistic regression beat the best gradient
    boosting pipeline</strong> (<code>{{best_boost_name}}</code>, PR-AUC {{best_boost_pr_auc}}): boosting minus logistic
    = {{boost_vs_lr_diff}} {{boost_vs_lr_ci}}. On this dataset the fraud odds multiply with each extra red flag
    ({{flag_odds_multipliers}} per flag), the additive log-odds shape a logistic model represents directly. That is a
    statement about this data, not about fraud models in general.</p>
  <p>The step-up threshold {{threshold}} was chosen on dev out-of-fold predictions only (per-repeat optimum
    {{thr_repeat_range}}). On CV it catches {{cv_recall}} of fraud while stepping up {{cv_alert_rate}} of
    transactions, about {{alerts_per_fraud}} step-ups per fraud found.</p>
</section>

<section id="simulator" class="wrap">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2><span class="num">4</span> Try the threshold</h2>
    <p>Move the threshold or change the cost assumptions. Everything is recomputed in your browser from the
      out-of-fold predictions on the dev split ({{dev_n}} transactions, {{dev_fraud}} fraud; counts averaged over the 3
      CV repeats). The holdout is not used here.</p>
  </div>
  <p class="callout" role="note"><strong>{{cost_label}}.</strong> The defaults are assumptions made for this project.
    With a bank&rsquo;s real costs the best threshold would have to be re-derived; scaling each cost &times;0.5 or
    &times;2 moves it between {{sens_thr_min}} and {{sens_thr_max}}.</p>

  <div class="sim" id="sim">
    <form class="sim-controls" id="sim-form" autocomplete="off">
      <div class="field field--threshold">
        <label for="thr">Step-up threshold <output id="thr-out" for="thr">{{threshold}}</output></label>
        <input type="range" id="thr" name="thr" min="0" max="1" step="1" value="0" disabled
               aria-describedby="thr-help">
        <p class="help" id="thr-help">Transactions scoring at or above this are stepped up. The slider moves along the
          threshold grid; the scale is roughly logarithmic.</p>
        <div class="buttons">
          <button type="button" id="btn-best" disabled>Lowest cost for these costs</button>
          <button type="button" id="btn-project" disabled>Project choice ({{threshold}})</button>
        </div>
        <p class="best-note" id="o-best"></p>
      </div>
      <fieldset class="costs">
        <legend>Cost assumptions <span class="muted">(illustrative, USD)</span></legend>
        <label class="field">Review / step-up cost per flagged transaction
          <input type="number" id="c-review" name="review_cost_per_alert" min="0" step="1" value="{{cost_review}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <label class="field">Extra friction when a good customer is stepped up
          <input type="number" id="c-friction" name="fp_friction_cost" min="0" step="1" value="{{cost_friction}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <label class="field">Share of a missed fraud&rsquo;s amount that is lost (0&ndash;2)
          <input type="number" id="c-fnfrac" name="fn_loss_fraction" min="0" max="2" step="0.1" value="{{cost_fn_frac}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <label class="field">Fixed handling cost per missed fraud
          <input type="number" id="c-fnfixed" name="fn_fixed_cost" min="0" step="1" value="{{cost_fn_fixed}}" inputmode="decimal" disabled></label>
        <button type="reset" id="btn-reset" disabled>Reset to project defaults</button>
        <p class="error" id="cost-error" role="alert" hidden></p>
      </fieldset>
    </form>

    <div class="sim-out" aria-live="polite">
      <ul class="sim-tiles">
        <li><span class="tile-label">Fraud caught (recall)</span><span class="tile-value" id="o-recall">{{cv_recall}}</span></li>
        <li><span class="tile-label">Step-ups that are fraud (precision)</span><span class="tile-value" id="o-precision">{{cv_precision}}</span></li>
        <li><span class="tile-label">Transactions stepped up</span><span class="tile-value" id="o-alert">{{cv_alert_rate}}</span></li>
        <li><span class="tile-label">Expected cost per 1,000 transactions</span><span class="tile-value" id="o-cost">{{cv_cost_per_1000}}</span>
          <span class="tile-sub" id="o-cost-sub">vs {{cv_nothing_per_1000}} stepping up nobody ({{cv_saving}} saved)</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="sim-sentence" id="o-sentence"></p>
      <figure class="chart">
        <figcaption><strong>Expected cost per 1,000 transactions vs threshold</strong> <span class="muted">(USD, log
          threshold axis; tap or click the chart to set the threshold)</span></figcaption>
        <div class="chart-box" id="cost-chart"></div>
        <div class="legend" aria-hidden="true">
          <span><i class="key key--line"></i>Model + threshold</span>
          <span><i class="key key--ref"></i>Step up nobody</span>
          <span><i class="key key--now"></i>Current threshold</span>
          <span><i class="key key--best"></i>Lowest cost</span>
        </div>
      </figure>
      <noscript><p class="muted">The simulator needs JavaScript. The values above are the project&rsquo;s choice
        (threshold {{threshold}}, default costs).</p></noscript>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="drivers" class="wrap prose">
  <h2><span class="num">5</span> What drives the score</h2>
  <p>Mean absolute SHAP value per feature on the dev split (log-odds units; exact linear SHAP for the logistic model,
    one-hot columns summed back to their source feature). The top three &mdash; {{shap_top3}} &mdash; carry
    {{shap_top3_share}} of the total.</p>
  {{shap_html}}
  <p>A false alarm and a caught fraud can carry the same red flags. Separating them more likely needs information
    this dataset does not have (customer history) than a different algorithm.</p>
</section>

<section id="didnt-work" class="wrap prose">
  <h2><span class="num">6</span> What didn&rsquo;t work (and why that is useful)</h2>
  <p>Every comparison was pre-registered: the simpler option is kept unless the 95% CI of the paired difference
    (corrected resampled t-test over 15 folds) excludes zero.</p>
  {{forest_html}}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SMOTE hurt badly</strong> ({{smote_diff}} {{smote_ci}}). SMOTENC fills categorical and boolean
      features by neighbour majority vote, so synthetic fraud carried red flags only {{smote_flag_ratio}}&times; as
      often as real fraud (IP-country mismatch: {{smote_ipmis_real}} of real fraud vs {{smote_ipmis_syn}} of synthetic).</li>
    <li><strong>Class weights and undersampling</strong> were not distinguishable from doing nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Calibration:</strong> {{calibration_verdict}}.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="card">
    <h3>A third tier (&ldquo;decline&rdquo;) did not clearly pay off</h3>
    <p>Approve / step-up / decline, with thresholds {{tt_stepup}} and {{tt_decline}} chosen on CV, looked cheaper
      on CV ({{tt_cv_diff}} per 1,000 transactions vs two tiers with the same model). On the holdout the difference
      was {{ho_tt_diff}} {{ho_tt_diff_ci}} per 1,000: the interval includes zero, so the simpler two-tier policy is
      recommended. <span class="muted">Assumes {{tt_pass_rate_pct}} of fraudsters pass step-up and a wrongly declined
      good customer costs {{tt_decline_fp}} USD. {{cost_label}}.</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="method" class="wrap prose">
  <h2>How the evaluation stays honest</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Holdout locked before any label-based analysis and scored once; a lock file refuses any other pipeline or
      threshold.</li>
    <li>All preprocessing and resampling are fitted inside training folds; leakage guards run in the test suite.</li>
    <li>Comparisons, metrics and decision rules were written down before the results (pre-registration with dated
      amendments).</li>
    <li>Every number on this page is generated from the project&rsquo;s committed result files; nothing is typed by
      hand.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

